{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_s2drbwmishntzhq23afewzmng4dkqqxfynhhcomg7ygohyok3h6q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All verification passes. Final summary:\n\n## Verification complete\n\nThe library is tiny (one C file, 50 lines, 2 exported functions) — a CWE out-of-bounds-write test case. All four phases pass under every configuration.\n\n**Phase A — artifacts** (`SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`)\n- `Cargo.toml` has **no `[features]`** and `CMakeLists.txt` has no `-D`/`#ifdef` → exactly **1 feature combination**. I additionally verified `release` separately since `[profile.release] panic = \"abort\"` is a real build-config difference.\n- C exports exactly `driver` + `printLine`; both are exported by the Rust `.so`. **Symbol diff empty**, 0 unexpected undefined symbols. No C module was left untranslated.\n\n**Phase B/C — 38 differential tests**, all driving *both* `.so`s via `libloading`/`dlsym` (never calling Rust directly): 18 `CONFIGS.md` rows (~4000 randomized inputs, fixed seed), 16 `ERRORS.md` rows, 4 symbol-parity tests. The `data < 0` out-of-bounds write is compared via `fork()`+`waitpid`, asserting the *same* termination signal.\n\n### Three real problems found and fixed in the test methodology\n\n1. **The suite was initially vacuous.** Because the crate is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` only, `cargo test` **does not rebuild the library** — my first mutation test (`'A'`→`'B'`) *passed* against a 9-minute-stale `.so`. Fixed with an `assert_fresh` staleness guard in the harness (fails loudly rather than silently passing) plus an explicit `cargo build` in `verify.sh`.\n2. **libtest's own progress output corrupted 6 rows.** Both functions write to process-wide `stdout`, so capture requires redirecting fd 1; libtest's `test foo ... ok` lines from other threads leaked into capture windows. Fixed by forcing `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` from an ELF `.init_array` constructor.\n3. **Verified the suite can actually detect a bad translation** — 10 mutants injected; 9 caught. Details in `ERRORS.md`.\n\n### Two honestly-documented observational-equivalence limits\n\n- **Sign- vs zero-extension of negative `data`** is *not* black-box observable: `(size_t)-1` = 2⁶⁴−1 and `(size_t)(unsigned)-1` = 2³²−1 both run off the stack with the same `SIGSEGV`. Distinguishing them would need >4 GiB of writable memory *above* a stack local — unobtainable. This mutant initially **survived**, so I added test `E8b`, which compares sign-extending instruction counts in `driver` (C: `movslq`+`cltq` = 2; Rust: `movslq`×2; mutant: 1). The translation's `data as usize` is faithful.\n- **`strncpy`'s NUL-stop/zero-pad path is unreachable** from the public API (`driver` only ever passes `n = data ≤ 99` and `source`'s NUL sits at index 99), and `strncpy_c` isn't exported — so that mutant is genuinely equivalent, not a coverage hole.\n\n**Conclusion: the Rust translation matches the C byte-for-byte on every tested input**, including the preserved vulnerability. Untouched: `c_src/` (only added `build/`) and `src/lib.rs` (verified byte-identical to the original after reverting all mutants). Re-run everything with `./verify.sh`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1219,"cache_read_input_tokens":119512,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1219,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f2c00af-bb63-4d71-bc98-8fa47b6dfbf9","uuid":"7b88fb9c-41a2-46ae-a338-b3fe11925aa1","timestamp":"2026-08-18T03:06:19.673Z"}
